2026-11-10  Timo Lassmann  <timolassmann@icloud.com>

	* version 4.0.0 - Two modes, fast (the default) and accurate, each with
	  a protein and a DNA/RNA setting fitted by grouped nested
	  cross-validation and scored on held-out data. First release since
	  3.6.0: it includes the unreleased 3.6.1 and 3.7.0 and the 4.0.0
	  pre-release entries below, whose mode settings are all replaced.
	- Modes: "fast" (single run; "default" is an alias) and "accurate"
	  (5-member ensemble with per-column confidence; "recall" is an
	  alias). The sequence type is detected from the input.
	- Settings (parameter key, pick file under presets/results):
	  protein fast 0976900c (fastfit/final_pick.json); protein accurate
	  be5c0b5a (ensconf/final_pick.json); DNA/RNA fast 64d6da0a
	  (nucfit/fast_final_pick.json); DNA/RNA accurate 7263e4b4
	  (nucfit/accurate_final_pick.json). Each is the final fit on all
	  training data. Fast: NSGA-III search, knee of the front. Accurate:
	  NSGA-III ensemble search, then a grid over guide-tree noise and
	  consensus support; the 5 members share member 1's (the fast
	  setting's) guide tree with seeded single-linkage tree noise (protein
	  0.05, DNA/RNA 0.2); consensus min_support 2 (a residue pair joins
	  two columns only when at least 2 members align it). Plans, amendments and records: presets/docs; the
	  fit code: presets/recipe.
	- lib/src/mode_presets.h is generated from the four pick files by
	  presets/release/emit_c_presets.py; tests/preset_golden.cmake checks
	  that the CLI reproduces the evaluated parameter path byte for byte
	  (protein and nucleotide, fast and accurate).
	- accurate refuses inputs above 5,000 sequences, protein and DNA/RNA
	  alike (KALIGN_ACCURATE_MAX_SEQ).
	- --type (CLI) / seq_type (Python): auto, protein, dna or rna. A
	  given type overrides detection and selects that type's settings;
	  a mismatch with detection gives a warning, not an error. Also
	  applies with --add. The values internal, divergent and pfasum*
	  are removed. Letters outside the chosen alphabet (e.g. protein
	  letters under --type dna) are scored as 'A' and reported in one
	  warning per input ("N characters not in the DNA/RNA alphabet (...)
	  were scored as 'A'"), not one warning per character.
	- New run option tree_sl_noise: seeded noise on the single-linkage
	  subtrees of the hybrid guide tree (default 0, no change).
	- Fix: kalign_msa_compare, _detailed and _with_mask paired the i-th
	  reference sequence with the i-th test sequence after sorting and
	  never checked that they were the same sequence; with extra, missing
	  or changed sequences they compared the wrong rows or read past the
	  end. Sequences are now matched by name and residues; extra test
	  sequences are ignored; a missing or changed reference sequence is an
	  error. Scores of matching inputs are unchanged. TC scoring keeps a
	  running residue count (same result, faster).
	- Fix: accurate mode with several threads crashed (SIGBUS) on macOS
	  for inputs with deep guide trees (e.g. 2,000 near-identical
	  sequences): the tree walks recursed once per tree level on 512 KB
	  worker stacks. The walks no longer recurse; worker stacks are 64 MB
	  (KALIGN_TP_STACK_SIZE). Output is unchanged.
	- OpenMP removed: the built-in thread pool (pthreads) is the only
	  parallel backend; the USE_OPENMP and USE_THREADPOOL CMake options
	  are gone. With -n 1 no worker thread is started. Supported
	  platforms: Linux and macOS. Output is unchanged.
	- Faster, output unchanged (byte-identical to the evaluated 4.0.0
	  code on all 14,475 paper alignments and 30,406 thread-identity
	  comparisons, x86-64 with and without AVX2; macOS/ARM test set
	  1,764/1,764). Consensus building in accurate mode: merge loop,
	  parallel no-op prefilter and radix sort, pair enumeration limited
	  to the support levels a pass needs, all ensemble members scored in
	  one pass that also counts the consensus candidates. Guide trees:
	  the LCS (protein) and Myers (DNA/RNA) distances run with a fixed
	  block count per sequence length, several pairs per vector (AVX2
	  on x86-64, NEON on ARM, scalar otherwise). Anchor positions
	  without a column map. Idle worker threads search 1000 times before
	  they park. Speed-up over the evaluated code on x86-64: accurate
	  protein 1.8-2.0x on 1 thread, 3.0-4.8x on 16; accurate DNA/RNA
	  1.6-1.9x; fast protein 1.1-2.5x; fast DNA/RNA 1.6x on 1 thread,
	  3.2x on 16.
	- Threads: at most the number of cores (sysconf); a larger -n or
	  n_threads is reduced with a warning (more threads than cores only
	  slowed the run down).
	- Fix: malformed input ended in a crash. One sequence, or a header
	  without sequence, freed the alignment twice; an empty sequence among
	  others crashed refinement (it is dropped again, as in 3.6.0); a
	  byte above 127 in sequences passed through the C API or Python
	  wrote outside the letter table (now an error). Empty input, an
	  unknown format and an unwritable output file give a clear error.
	  Python exceptions carry the reason from the library
	  (kalign_last_error, kalign_clear_error in the C API).
	- Fix: data race on the VSM debug flag in accurate mode (harmless,
	  reported by ThreadSanitizer).
	- Python source package: only what building and testing needs (12 MB
	  -> 0.36 MB); experiments, preset fitting, benchmarks and development
	  notes are left out.
	- Options follow the modes, on the command line and in Python. Gap
	  penalties (--gpo, --gpe, --tgpe; gap_open, gap_extend,
	  terminal_gap_extend), the VSM options and --guide-dist change one
	  alignment and are accepted with fast only; with accurate they are
	  an error ("--gpo cannot be used with --mode accurate: the accurate
	  ensemble uses its fitted settings"). The fill-in options are
	  accepted with accurate only. Before, the Python package ran fast
	  without notice when a gap penalty was given with accurate, and the
	  command line applied the value to all five members.
	- Command line: numbers are checked ("--gpo abc" and "-n 0" are
	  errors); a run without input exits with status 1; output format
	  names are matched exactly and case-insensitively (fasta, fa, afa,
	  msf, clu, clustal); --add and --existing must be given together;
	  the help text lists options by mode; confidence options with fast
	  give a warning (confidence needs accurate).
	- C library: kalign() runs fast mode with the fitted settings (same
	  signature; a gap penalty below 0 keeps the fitted value). New
	  kalign_align_mode(msa, mode, seq_type, n_threads) runs a named
	  mode like the command line. Library, command line and Python give
	  the same alignment for the same mode (tests/kalign_unity_test.c).
	- MSF output of protein alignments has a protein header.
	- Python: default 1 thread (n_threads= or set_num_threads() for
	  more), documented; the low-level _core functions are documented as
	  a development interface. benchmarks/downstream removed.
	- Fix (Linux): the Python module's error path called glibc's error()
	  and ended the interpreter (library functions error and basename
	  renamed or removed); thread-local DP buffers are freed when a
	  thread ends (lib/src/tl_scratch.c); spare sequence entries were
	  leaked when empty sequences were dropped.
	- Build: Windows/MSVC leftovers removed; the zig build enables AVX2
	  on x86-64, like CMake (an AVX2 CPU is required; use CMake with
	  -DENABLE_AVX2=OFF otherwise).
	- Library ABI: SOVERSION 4 (as 3.7.0).

