2026-10-08  Timo Lassmann  <timolassmann@icloud.com>

	* 4.0.0 pre-release (not released; its modes were replaced, see
	  2026-11-10) - Two modes per sequence type, fast and accurate,
	  chosen by pooled nested cross-validation (presets/results/v2)
	- Modes: "fast" and "accurate"; "default" and "recall" are aliases of
	  "accurate", which is now the default. Protein accurate is a 5-run
	  ensemble; nucleotide accurate is a single run with consistency
	  anchors and realignment.
	- The parameters are generated into lib/src/mode_presets.h from
	  presets/results/v2/presets_v2.json by
	  presets/release/emit_c_presets.py; the CLI and the Python mode API
	  reproduce the evaluated parameter path byte for byte
	  (tests/preset_golden.cmake, presets/release/equiv_check.py).
	- Library ABI unchanged (SOVERSION 4).
	- Accurate (ensemble) mode memory: the per-sequence-pair POAR table is
	  replaced by per-run residue-to-column maps; pairs are derived on
	  demand. Peak memory 2-9% of before at 1,000 sequences, 17-26% faster,
	  output byte-identical; no 32-bit overflow above 46,340 sequences.
	- Fix: stack overflow with many sequences and threads (e.g. fast mode at
	  200,000 sequences): waiting threads nested unrelated tasks without
	  bound; foreign-task nesting is now capped (threadpool). Output unchanged.
	- Fix: in accurate mode, residues x and x + 4096 of a sequence longer
	  than 4096 residues shared a POAR key, so their pair support was
	  merged. Keys are now 64-bit; output changes only for inputs with such
	  a sequence. POAR files are written as version 2 (version 1 still read).
	- Fix: memory leak on consensus-building error paths.
	- Removed the --load-poar command-line option (no Kalign command writes POAR
	  files any more) and --min-support, which only that path used;
	  kalign_consensus_from_poar stays in the C API.

2026-10-03  Timo Lassmann  <timolassmann@icloud.com>

	* version 3.7.0 - New protein default/accurate presets; new alignment
	  mechanisms; DP correctness fixes; 2-3x faster; library SOVERSION 4
	- Includes 3.6.1 (never released separately; see below).
	- Speed-ups (they leave the output byte-identical on the same
	  platform: BAliBASE 3 (218 cases x 3 modes), BRAliBASE (481 x 4
	  modes), simulated sets and tests/data in all modes, 1 to 16
	  threads, on Apple M3 (NEON) and AMD Threadripper 3970X (AVX2)):
	  - Guide tree: the sequence-to-anchor BPM distances computed while
	    picking anchors are reused instead of recomputed, and each pair of
	    a leaf cluster is computed once instead of twice.
	  - BPM: the match table is built in one pass over the pattern; the
	    block step is branch-free; a SIMD block wavefront (NEON from 6
	    blocks, AVX2 from 2 blocks) processes several 64-bit blocks at once.
	  - Ensembles: POAR extraction merges each sequence pair's residue
	    pairs in one pass, and scoring scans instead of binary-searching.
	  - DP: wavefront SIMD forward/backward passes for seq-seq,
	    seq-profile and profile-profile alignment (lib/src/aln_wave.c,
	    generated from perf/dp/kernels; NEON and AVX2 through
	    lib/src/wave_simd.h), on by default (CMake option KALIGN_WAVE);
	    builds without NEON/AVX2 and MSVC use the scalar kernels.
	  - Threads: refinement (default mode) runs the two subtrees of every
	    guide-tree node concurrently, its DP passes run forward/backward in
	    parallel, and its trials run concurrently; parallel loops use 4
	    chunks per thread so the pairwise (j > i) loops balance.  Default
	    mode at 8-32 threads goes from 1.3-1.7x to 2.4-7.4x over one thread
	    (x86, simulated N50-N2000); accurate 15-35% faster at 4+ threads.
	  - perf/dp: profiling and DP benchmark harness (KALIGN_PERF, off by
	    default) used to develop and verify the kernels.
	  - Single thread, vs the same code without them: x86 fast 2.89x,
	    default 2.45x, accurate 2.70x (BAliBASE accurate 1003 s ->
	    318 s); M3 fast 2.39x, default 1.91x, accurate 2.03x.
	  - Note: ARM and x86 builds can differ in near-tie cases because the
	    profile-profile score is a fused multiply-add on AArch64 and a
	    separate multiply and add on x86 without FMA (BAliBASE: 9/218
	    fast, 8/218 default, 51/218 accurate outputs differ; F1/TC
	    differences under 0.001).
	- New mechanisms (all opt-in through kalign_run_config /
	  kalign_ensemble_config, CLI flags and the Python bindings):
	  - Per-merge variable scoring matrix (vsm_mode = 1, --vsm-mode,
	    --vsm-kappa, --vsm-dzero): the substitution offset is computed
	    for each profile merge from the distance between the two groups
	    and bounded by the gap penalties. vsm_mode = 0 keeps the legacy
	    VSM.
	  - Guide-tree distances from pairwise global alignments
	    (guide_dist = 1, --guide-dist 1): Kimura (protein) or
	    Jukes-Cantor (DNA/RNA) corrected distances with the run's matrix
	    and gap penalties. guide_dist = 0 keeps the BPM edit distance.
	  - Consensus fill-in for ensembles (fill_in, --fill-in,
	    --fill-anchor-frac, --fill-support): residues the POAR consensus
	    leaves in columns of their own can be realigned between anchor
	    columns, projected from the best run, or attached to an existing
	    column.
	  - Input validation for the new CLI and Python parameters; state is
	    restored on VSM error paths.
	- Correctness fixes in the DP kernels (seq-seq, seq-profile and
	  profile-profile; these change the output of every mode):
	  - A match entering or leaving a terminal row gap at the far end of
	    the forward (last column) or backward (first column) pass was not
	    charged the gap-open penalty that every other terminal-gap
	    transition pays, so a gap's price depended on which Hirschberg
	    half it fell in and on argument order.
	  - The Hirschberg meetup priced a row gap continuing across the
	    split row with the terminal extension at every column whenever
	    the sub-problem started in column 0; interior columns now pay the
	    gap extension and terminal columns nothing (both halves already
	    carry those rows' cost).
	  - The backward profile kernels charged gap extension with the
	    profile slot of the previous gap residue instead of the residue
	    being added, as the forward kernels, the profile setup and the
	    path scoring do.
	  - The forward kernels looked up the consistency bonus one column
	    off (the bonus for pair a_i, b_k was credited to a_i, b_(k-1));
	    this affects modes with consistency anchors (3.7.0 default).
	  With the fixes the forward total equals the backward total and an
	  independent full-matrix DP, the returned path is optimal, and
	  aligning (A,B) and (B,A) gives the same score, on 60,000 random
	  problems and on 216 problems captured from real runs, with the
	  consistency bonus on and off; before the fixes about 10-15% of
	  random paths were suboptimal and 14-23% depended on argument order.
	  Measured effect on alignment quality (60 BAliBASE cases, 192 WAG
	  simulations; development data) is within +/-0.006 on SP,
	  precision, F1 and TC in every mode (e.g. default F1 +0.004
	  BAliBASE, +0.002 WAG).
	- Changed protein presets (confirmed on held-out data before
	  adoption; 3.6.1 = shipped):
	  - default: new single-run preset (consistency anchors, realign,
	    refinement, lower VSM amplitude).
	  - accurate: new 5-run ensemble (per-merge VSM, pairwise-alignment
	    guide trees in 3 of 5 runs, min_support 2).
	  - recall: now the SAME preset as accurate; for protein input
	    recall is an alias of accurate.
	  - fast: unchanged (the candidate missed the tree-accuracy
	    non-inferiority margin by 0.0009, so the shipped preset is kept).
	  - Nucleotide (DNA/RNA) presets: unchanged.
	- Held-out confirmation, 192 simulated LG alignments (co-primary
	  outcomes; difference new - 3.6.1, nRF lower is better; measured on
	  the code before the DP correctness fixes above):
	  - default:  TC +0.047, F1 +0.030, nRF -0.009 (not significant).
	  - recall:   nRF -0.047, TC +0.027, F1 +0.014.
	  - accurate: nRF -0.083, TC +0.046, F1 -0.0045 (a real loss, within
	    the pre-registered -0.010 margin).
	  PREFAB 4 (1390 cases), Q: default +0.079, accurate +0.064,
	  recall +0.0005.
	  Re-measured with the released code (fixes and speed-ups, same
	  presets): LG sims default nRF 0.183, TC 0.699, F1 0.762;
	  accurate/recall nRF 0.164, TC 0.712, F1 0.784; fast nRF 0.196,
	  TC 0.642, F1 0.704. PREFAB 4 Q: default 0.639, accurate 0.655,
	  fast 0.636. Single-thread CPU (M3 Max): LG 2 / 12 / 54 s and
	  PREFAB 23 / 117 / 769 s for fast / default / accurate (MAFFT
	  L-INS-i 132 s and 1,121 s).
	- Trade-off: the new default and accurate/recall presets favour
	  recall, column accuracy (TC) and the accuracy of trees inferred
	  from the alignment. The high precision of the old accurate preset
	  is not retained (LG simulations: precision 0.883 -> 0.777, recall
	  0.743 -> 0.793; PREFAB 4 precision 0.781 -> 0.668). With the
	  speed-ups the new default takes about 1.2x the CPU time of the old
	  one (LG 12 vs 10 s, PREFAB 117 vs 101 s); accurate takes about half
	  (54 vs 141 s, 769 vs 1,431 s).
	- Fill-in: the confirmed ensemble was selected with fill_in = attach
	  and fill_support 5; with min_support 2 attach never applies, so the
	  outputs are byte-identical to fill-in off. The preset uses
	  fill_in = off.
	- The C presets reproduce the confirmed parameter sets byte for byte
	  through the Python ensemble path (new ctest kalign_preset_golden;
	  golden hashes regenerated with tests/preset_golden_regen.py after
	  the DP correctness fixes, so they differ from the alignments of
	  the confirmation run).
	- ABI: kalign_run_config and kalign_ensemble_config gained fields
	  and kalign_run_config_defaults() / kalign_ensemble_config_defaults()
	  return them by value, so the C ABI changed. The shared library
	  SOVERSION is now set independently of the project version and is 4
	  (libkalign.so.4); code linked against earlier 3.x libraries must be
	  recompiled.

2026-09-30  Timo Lassmann  <timolassmann@icloud.com>

	* version 3.6.1 - Bug fix: ensemble consensus could scramble residues
	- Fixed a bug in ensemble consensus building (recall and accurate
	  modes) that could reorder residues within output sequences. The
	  column-ordering cycle check used a fixed 4096-entry queue and
	  silently stopped searching when it filled; the missed cycle was then
	  broken by the final column sort, placing residues out of order.
	  Affected inputs are those with wide consensus alignments (> ~4096
	  columns): on BAliBASE 3, 2/218 cases in accurate mode and 3/218 in
	  recall mode returned scrambled sequences. Output sequences are now
	  checked to occupy strictly increasing columns.
	- Consensus columns are now ordered with an online topological order
	  (Pearce-Kelly), which also makes consensus building much faster on
	  large inputs (e.g. 493 sequences: accurate mode 47.8 s -> 8.9 s).
	- Output changes: fast and default modes are byte-identical to 3.6.0.
	  In recall and accurate modes the aligned residue pairs are identical
	  except where 3.6.0 produced corrupted output; columns that are not
	  ordered by any sequence may appear in a different order. In recall
	  mode a correct consensus can now outscore the single best run where
	  the corrupted one did not (5/218 BAliBASE cases).
	- n_threads = 1 now runs on one thread (previously the thread pool
	  added a worker thread next to the calling thread). Output unchanged.
	- New regression test: consensus preserves residue order
	  (kalign_ensemble_test_long on BB40023).

2026-05-16  Timo Lassmann  <timolassmann@icloud.com>

	* version 3.6.0 - Drop Python 3.9/3.10 support; CI Node 24 upgrade
	- Minimum Python version raised to 3.11. Python 3.9 reached EOL in
	  October 2025 and 3.10 reaches EOL in October 2026; both are now
	  beyond their supported lifecycle for upstream wheels (pillow,
	  matplotlib). Wheels and CI matrices now target 3.11 / 3.12 / 3.13
	  / 3.14.
	- GitHub Actions updated to Node.js 24 successor versions
	  (checkout v5, setup-python v6, upload/download-artifact v5,
	  codecov v5) ahead of the 2026-06-02 forced Node 24 switch.
	- Drive-by: dropped redundant `brew install cmake` on macOS-latest
	  runners (cmake is preinstalled).

	* version 3.5.2 - Four-mode preset system, threadpool, hardening
	Algorithm and presets
	- Four mode presets stable for protein and nucleotide:
	  fast, default, recall, accurate. Per-mode configurations
	  derived by NSGA-III multi-objective optimisation on
	  BAliBASE v4 (protein) and BRAliBASE (RNA).
	- Unified nucleotide preset (DNA and RNA share one path).
	- Ensemble alignment with POAR consensus and per-column /
	  per-residue confidence scores.
	- New --add mode: append sequences to an existing alignment.
	- Sparse consistency-bonus matrix for large MSAs (avoids
	  quadratic memory in column count).
	- Removed deprecated 'precise' mode alias (Python only;
	  never shipped as a wheel).

	Parallelism
	- Chase-Lev work-stealing thread pool is now the default
	  parallelism backend; OpenMP is optional (USE_OPENMP=ON).
	- macOS wheels no longer link libomp.dylib, resolving the
	  conda-forge / numpy OpenMP runtime conflict.

	Security / robustness
	- POAR file parsing now rejects malformed inputs that would
	  cause integer overflow in pair-count or per-pair entry
	  count (lib/src/poar.c). Limited threat model (requires
	  explicit --load-poar) but tightened anyway.
	- finalise_alignment is now atomic w.r.t. msa->sequences:
	  validates all per-sequence linear lengths before swapping
	  any pointer, so a failure leaves the MSA unchanged.
	- kalign_msa_compare wraps finalise_alignment in RUN(); a
	  failure surfaces cleanly instead of producing a half-
	  finalised MSA.
	- DSSIM stress test now uses KALIGN_MATRIX_AUTO so it
	  exercises both protein and DNA biotypes correctly.
	- Bumped locked dependencies (cryptography, flask, werkzeug,
	  pillow, pygments, pytest, urllib3, requests, mako, black)
	  past their CVE fix versions.

	Build / tooling
	- build.zig updated for zig 0.16 (four cross-compile targets).
	- tests/check-local.sh: one-command pre-push gate that runs
	  zig build + native ctest + Linux ASAN container + pytest.
	- Containerfile.memcheck (Ubuntu + ASAN + Valgrind) for local
	  Linux memory-bug reproduction.
	- Removed paper-side benchmark machinery, optimizer scripts,
	  and design-rationale PRDs from the public tree (preserved
	  in the manuscript repository for reproducibility).
	- Cleaned up ~550 lines of dead code (coretralign,
	  bitShiftRight256ymm, unused split() in bisectingKmeans).
	- Build is now warning-free on clang and GCC.
	- Dropped the broken benchmark CI workflow (BAliBASE download
	  endpoint has been gone for months).

	* version 3.5.1 - Bugfix release
	- Fix memory leak in build_tree_from_pairwise (realign/ensemble)
	- Move seaborn from core to optional dependency
	- Fix benchmark workflow to handle unavailable datasets gracefully
	- Black formatting fixes

	* version 3.5.0 - Three modes
	Kalign now has three modes: default (best general-purpose), fast
	(same as v3.4), and precise (ensemble, highest accuracy, ~10x slower).
	- Ensemble alignment (--precise or --ensemble N)
	- Per-column confidence scores from ensemble mode
	- Ensemble consensus save/load (--save-poar, --load-poar)
	- Alignment refinement (--refine)
	- PFASUM substitution matrices (--type pfasum, pfasum43, pfasum60)
	- stdin via -i - convention (samtools/bcftools style)
	- Python align() now supports all parameters (mode, ensemble, etc.)
	- License changed from GPL-3.0-or-later to Apache-2.0
	- Fixed crash in Python align() on Linux (uninitialized MSA fields)
	- Fixed selenocysteine (U) handling in reduced alphabet (Debian #1127766)

